  "num": 3,
  "title": "They Tell You What to Care About. I'll Tell You What Actually Matters.",
  "slug": "they-tell-you-what-to-care-about-i-ll-tell-you-what-actually-matters",
  "category": "Politics & Power",
  "subcategory": "America First vs. Both Parties",
  "image": "assets/blog/post3.jpg",
  "paragraphs": [
   "Every single day, the news tells you what you're supposed to care about. The country you're supposed to worry about. The issue that's supposed to keep you up at night.",
   "Well, I don't know about you, but I'm sick and tired of hearing about the Strait of Hormuz.",
   "The Strait of Hormuz. A place I've never been, a place I'll never go, and a place I should never have to pay for. But turn on any cable network and that's the story, hour after hour, expert after expert, all of them very concerned and none of them talking about the thing hitting your actual life.",
   "Here's what they're not covering. The price of beef at your grocery store. The family of four that can't afford health insurance. The rancher in Texas watching a parasite spread across his herd while Washington calls it somebody else's problem. What's happening in your town, on your road, at your kitchen table.",
   "Why? Because the news is obsessed with whatever Washington is obsessed with. And Washington is always — always — focused somewhere else. Some other country. Some other crisis. Anywhere but here.",
   "The issue I care about is the United States of America. The American people. And what is actually plaguing us. Not a shipping lane on the other side of the planet that I'll never see but somehow end up paying for.",
   "I learned something the longer I was in politics. The stories they push hardest are usually the ones that keep you from looking at the stories they'd rather you ignore. Follow what they want you distracted from, and you'll usually find what actually matters.",
   "So here's my rule, and I'd offer it to you. When the whole machine is screaming at you to care about one thing, ask yourself what you'd be noticing if they weren't. Ask what's happening right where you live that nobody with a microphone wants to talk about.",
   "Then go find out for yourself. Do your own research. Because the people telling you what to care about have never once had your interests at heart."
  ],
