  "title": "Three Countries in Two Days: A Little Behind-the-Scenes",
  "slug": "three-countries-in-two-days-a-little-behind-the-scenes",
  "category": "Life & Family",
  "subcategory": "Personal Story / Behind-the-scenes",
  "image": "assets/blog/post5.jpg",
  "paragraphs": [
   "People see the fighter. The clips, the hearings, the arguments. What they don't usually see is the ordinary stuff — the travel, the marriage, the tired airport mornings. So let me pull the curtain back a little, because the ordinary stuff is where you actually get to know a person.",
   "Not long ago, Brian and I did three countries in two days. We woke up in Costa Rica, drove to the airport, flew to Houston, spent the night, had a great steak dinner, and flew on to Puerto Vallarta the next morning. By the time we landed I'd lost track of what day it was. If you've ever done that kind of back-to-back travel, you know it's equal parts exciting and exhausting — a lot more glamorous in the photos than it is at 5 a.m. in a security line.",
   "I'll tell you a small moment that made me laugh. Going through customs, they asked if we were bringing in any fruits or vegetables. Nope. I told them I was coming to eat the fruits and vegetables grown down here — not carry in the garbage from somewhere else. Brian and I got a kick out of that. It's a little thing, but it's the kind of little thing that reminds you to pay attention to what you're actually putting in your body, wherever you are.",
   "Here's what these trips remind me of every time. I do what I do — the fighting, the speaking, the refusing to shut up — because I love this country and the people in it. But you cannot pour from an empty cup. You have to rest. You have to laugh with the person you love. You have to get out of the noise long enough to remember why you're in it.",
   "Brian and I are a team. We travel together, we make decisions together, and we take care of our health together. A lot of what keeps me steady when the world is loud isn't a strategy or a talking point. It's having someone beside me who knows the real me, not the one on television.",
   "So this is the part of Life with MTG that isn't a fight. It's just life — a little travel, a little laughter, a lot of gratitude. I'll keep bringing you the battles, because there are plenty of them. But every now and then I want to bring you this too, because I think you're tired of only ever seeing people at war.",
   "More soon. Thanks for coming along."
  ],
