  "title": "Who Profits When Washington Says It's \"Safe and Effective\"?",
  "slug": "who-profits-when-washington-says-it-s-safe-and-effective",
  "category": "Politics & Power",
  "subcategory": "Follow the Money",
  "image": "assets/blog/post6.jpg",
  "paragraphs": [
   "I want to give you a little background, because once you understand how this actually works, you'll never hear the phrase \"safe and effective\" the same way again.",
   "When I served on the committee overseeing the government's COVID response, I learned something most Americans have no idea about. Government scientists — the same experts whose agencies tell you what's safe, all drawing taxpayer-funded paychecks — can hold patents on medications and technologies their own agencies then approve and promote. They collect royalties on those products. Taxpayer-funded research, taxpayer-funded salaries, and then personal royalties on top.",
   "Now I want to be careful here, because I'm not a scientist and I won't pretend to be one. What I'm telling you is documented — government scientists holding patents and collecting royalties is a real, verified practice. Whether that constitutes a conflict of interest in any specific approval decision is something reasonable people can debate. But I'll tell you what it looks like from where I sit: it looks like the people telling you what's safe have a financial interest in you believing them.",
   "Follow the money. It's the simplest rule in politics and it applies here like it applies everywhere else. When someone tells you to trust them, ask what they gain from your trust. When an agency says a product is safe, ask whether anyone at that agency profits from the product being on the market.",
   "I'm not telling you to reject medicine or ignore doctors. I'm telling you to ask questions — the same questions you'd ask about any politician, any lobbyist, any deal that seems too good to be true. Who benefits? Who pays? And who's making the rules?",
   "The pharmaceutical industry is among the largest donors to the politicians who oversee the agencies that approve their products. That's not a conspiracy theory. That's public record. And once you see that loop — industry funds politicians, politicians oversee agencies, agencies approve industry products, scientists collect royalties — you understand why \"safe and effective\" deserves a harder look than we've been giving it.",
   "Do your own research. Look at the royalty disclosures. Look at the lobbying numbers. Look at who sits on which boards after they leave government. Then decide for yourself how much blind trust that system has earned."
  ],
  "date": "Jun 20, 2026",
